# Cloudflare Pages headers.

# EVERY PAGE, and the reason it matters more than a static brochure suggests.
#
# /reset is not a brochure. Supabase puts a password-recovery token in the URL
# and that page reads `window.location.hash` (and, when Supabase uses the query
# string instead, `window.location.search`) to hand it to the app through a
# `samewhere://` deep link. /i does the same job for invite links. So this
# origin handles credentials in URLs, on pages that also run inline script,
# and it was serving them with no security headers at all.
#
# The policy is written to be strict by DEFAULT and to open only what the pages
# actually use, which is very little: across the whole site there is exactly one
# external reference (an apps.apple.com App Store LINK, not a subresource),
# no fonts, no analytics, no third-party anything.
/*
  # default-src 'none' is the whole policy; everything below is an exception.
  # connect-src is deliberately absent, so it inherits 'none': a script on this
  # origin cannot fetch, XHR or WebSocket a recovery token anywhere. That is the
  # exfiltration path this header exists to close.
  #
  # 'unsafe-inline' on script-src is honest rather than lazy: three pages carry
  # an inline <script> and there is no build step here to hash them, so a hash
  # would silently stop the reset page working the first time somebody edited a
  # comment in it. It costs little here because these files are STATIC - no user
  # input is reflected into any of them, so there is no injection point for
  # 'unsafe-inline' to widen. An attacker who can add script to these pages has
  # already replaced the files.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'unsafe-inline'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  # No page here is ever meant to be framed, and /reset in a frame is a
  # clickjacking target. frame-ancestors above is the modern spelling; this is
  # the one older browsers obey.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # The recovery token rides in the fragment, which is never sent in a Referer -
  # but Supabase sometimes uses the QUERY STRING instead, and that is. no-referrer
  # is the only setting that cannot leak it.
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  # Nothing on this site needs a sensor, and hard rule 7 says the app never
  # asks for live location. Saying so in a header costs nothing and means a
  # future page cannot quietly start.
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()

# The association file has no extension, so nothing infers its type. iOS
# refuses to parse it unless it arrives as application/json, and the failure is
# silent: links simply open Safari and nobody is told why.
/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
